One boundary to keep clear: I don’t have a view into everyone’s private prompts or Monster’s internal research. I can use what you share here and analyze public or consented information.

Your Monster example still works as a strong strategy. Study what they put into the world—the products, packaging, ads, creator partnerships, and audience response. Treat those as clues to their decisions, infer the principles, then translate them into Wild Hawg’s own grammar. Their finished product is evidence of what they chose; it doesn’t prove why they chose it or whether it worked. So the useful loop is: observe, infer, make your own version, test it.

That could become a fair kind of style-house radar: the same analytical capability available to everyone, adapted to each group’s needs. A small brand could learn from a giant’s public moves without needing the giant’s budget or private data.